\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Swadesh convergence ($y$-axis, ABTT-corrected $k=3$) vs.\ cross-lingual surface-form similarity across all 92{} Latin-script languages in our sample. (a) Mean orthographic similarity ($1 - \text  {NLD}$ on raw word forms) explains $R^2 = 0.012{}$ ($p = 0.272{}$). (b) Mean phonological similarity ($1 - \text  {NLD}$ after voicing/diacritic normalization) explains $R^2 = 0.000{}$ ($p = 0.929{}$). Over $98.7\%$ of the variance in Swadesh convergence is independent of surface-form overlap.}}{16}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:swadesh}{{9}{16}{Swadesh convergence ($y$-axis, ABTT-corrected $k=3$) vs.\ cross-lingual surface-form similarity across all \DecompNumLatinLangs {} Latin-script languages in our sample. (a) Mean orthographic similarity ($1 - \text {NLD}$ on raw word forms) explains $R^2 = \DecompRsqOrtho {}$ ($p = \DecompPOrtho {}$). (b) Mean phonological similarity ($1 - \text {NLD}$ after voicing/diacritic normalization) explains $R^2 = \DecompRsqPhon {}$ ($p = \DecompPPhon {}$). Over $98.7\%$ of the variance in Swadesh convergence is independent of surface-form overlap}{figure.caption.15}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Controlling for loanword and part-of-speech confounds in Swadesh vs.\ non-Swadesh comparisons (39{}-language subset). (a) When matched on part of speech and low orthographic overlap, Swadesh core concrete nouns ($n = 52{}$, $\mu = 0.83{}$) achieve significantly higher cross-lingual convergence than controlled non-Swadesh concrete nouns ($n = 60{}$, $\mu = 0.78{}$; Mann-Whitney $U = 2016{}$, $p = 0.004{}$, Cohen's $d = 0.45{}$ raw; $U = 2099{}$, $p = 8.40 \times 10^{-4}{}$, $d = 0.54{}$ after ABTT correction; $d = 0.69{}$ on strictly tangible nouns). (b) Mean cross-lingual orthographic similarity ($1 - \text  {NLD}$) confirms that modern international loanwords ($n = 60{}$, e.g., \emph  {coffee, radio, internet}) have twice the surface-form overlap ($0.26{}$) of both Swadesh core nouns ($0.12{}$) and controlled non-Swadesh nouns ($0.13{}$).}}{17}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:swadesh_comp}{{10}{17}{Controlling for loanword and part-of-speech confounds in Swadesh vs.\ non-Swadesh comparisons (\LayerwiseNumLangs {}-language subset). (a) When matched on part of speech and low orthographic overlap, Swadesh core concrete nouns ($n = \PosNounSwadeshCount {}$, $\mu = \PosNounSwadeshMean {}$) achieve significantly higher cross-lingual convergence than controlled non-Swadesh concrete nouns ($n = \ControlledSwadeshCompNumNonSwadesh {}$, $\mu = \PosNounNonSwadeshMean {}$; Mann-Whitney $U = \PosNounCompU {}$, $p = \PosNounCompP {}$, Cohen's $d = \PosNounCompCohenD {}$ raw; $U = \PosNounCorrCompU {}$, $p = \PosNounCorrCompP {}$, $d = \PosNounCorrCompCohenD {}$ after ABTT correction; $d = \StrictNounCorrCohenD {}$ on strictly tangible nouns). (b) Mean cross-lingual orthographic similarity ($1 - \text {NLD}$) confirms that modern international loanwords ($n = \SwadeshCompNumNonSwadesh {}$, e.g., \emph {coffee, radio, internet}) have twice the surface-form overlap ($\OrthoSimLoanword {}$) of both Swadesh core nouns ($\OrthoSimSwadeshNouns {}$) and controlled non-Swadesh nouns ($\OrthoSimControlled {}$)}{figure.caption.16}{}}
