\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Cross-linguistic colexification structure across all $\binom  {101}{2} = 5050{}$ Swadesh concept pairs mapped to CLICS\textsuperscript  {3} \citep  {list2018,rzymski2020}. (a) Mean within-language concept cosine similarity as a function of colexification frequency (number of distinct language families expressing both concepts with a single word form), stratified by semantic domain: within-category colexified pairs ($n = 399{}$) are colored by domain, cross-category colexified pairs ($n = 1{,}393{}$, e.g., \emph  {bark--skin, big--many, ear--hear}) are shown as slate diamonds, and never-colexified pairs ($n = 3258{}$) as grey points. Restricting to within-category pairs ($n = 783{}$, solid red fit) nearly triples the continuous rank correlation ($\rho _s = 0.32{}$, $p = 2.73 \times 10^{-20}{}$) relative to the unstratified fit ($\rho _s = 0.13{}$, $p = 8.85 \times 10^{-21}{}$, dashed grey fit). (b) Mean cosine similarity ($\pm $SEM) across colexification tiers ($\ge 3$, $1$--$2$, and $0$ families) and semantic domains, showing consistent monotonic ordering within every category.}}{21}{figure.caption.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:colex}{{13}{21}{Cross-linguistic colexification structure across all $\binom {\NumConcepts }{2} = \ColexNumPairs {}$ Swadesh concept pairs mapped to CLICS\textsuperscript {3} \citep {list2018,rzymski2020}. (a) Mean within-language concept cosine similarity as a function of colexification frequency (number of distinct language families expressing both concepts with a single word form), stratified by semantic domain: within-category colexified pairs ($n = \ColexWithinCatColexCount {}$) are colored by domain, cross-category colexified pairs ($n = \ColexCrossCatColexCount {}$, e.g., \emph {bark--skin, big--many, ear--hear}) are shown as slate diamonds, and never-colexified pairs ($n = \ColexNonColCount {}$) as grey points. Restricting to within-category pairs ($n = \ColexWithinCatPairs {}$, solid red fit) nearly triples the continuous rank correlation ($\rho _s = \ColexWithinCatRho {}$, $p = \ColexWithinCatRhoP {}$) relative to the unstratified fit ($\rho _s = \ColexSpearmanRho {}$, $p = \ColexSpearmanP {}$, dashed grey fit). (b) Mean cosine similarity ($\pm $SEM) across colexification tiers ($\ge 3$, $1$--$2$, and $0$ families) and semantic domains, showing consistent monotonic ordering within every category}{figure.caption.19}{}}
